<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nokalakevi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Archaeopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nokalakevi is an archaeological site in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, identified with Archaeopolis, the chief fortress and capital of the kingdom of Lazica in late antiquity. Its walls, gates and churches date mainly from the 4th to 6th centuries, when it was a key stronghold during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazic-war">Lazic War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, who failed to take it in 551. Georgian and British excavations have uncovered layers going back to the Colchian period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nokalakevi.jpg" alt="Nokalakevi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lika (ლიკა) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nokalakevi99.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
